>If you're using assembly, you must want speed and/or very small code. If
>this is the case, then why on earth are you even bothering with a compiled
>language? If it's not, then why on earth are you even bothering with
>assembly?
>

The reason is that time-critical routines can be written and hand-optimized in
assembly, and non-time-critical routines can be written in the high-level-
language of your choice.  A QUITE common practice.

Another reason is that in certain high-level languages, certain operations are
not made available.  For example, bitwise AND, OR, and XOR are not available 
in standard Pascal (they may be found in Turbo; I'm not sure.)  These are 
VERY important in game programming.  It is a simple task to write a Pascal 
function in assembly which efficiently implements these (try writing in using
only standard Pascal code).  Conversely, floating point can be a MAJOR pain to
do in assembly, especially if you use double precision, which will not fit into
a single 32-bit register.  In short, there are MANY good reasons to combine 
high-level languages and assembly.  A good programmer, like a good carpenter,
painter, cook, or any other artist, will have a number of tools at his/her 
disposal, and know when to use these correctly.

There are many good books available which cover interfacing assembly with 
high-level-languages (such as C or Pascal).  Unfortunately, I can't remember
off the top of my head.  I'll post some titles when I get home (If I don't 
forget......)


